  {
   "id": "choosing-years",
   "nav": "Choosing years",
   "kicker": "Method question",
   "title": "How professionals choose tuning and test years",
   "paragraphs": [
    {
     "technical": "One fixed split (tune 2010–2022, test 2023+) rests on a single guess, and a 12-year tuning block spans several regimes. The standard answer is **walk-forward analysis**: tune on a window, test on the next unseen year, slide forward one year, repeat. Every year after the first window becomes an out-of-sample year exactly once; the stitched test years are the record. Stricter variants: **purged / combinatorial cross-validation** (López de Prado) tests every block against the rest with an embargo gap, and **regime-split tests** report trending, ranging and volatile periods separately.",
     "plain": "Instead of picking one set of years to practise on and one to test on, you practise on a few years, test on the next one, then move everything forward a year and repeat. Every year gets tested once on settings chosen only from the years before it — the closest thing to having actually run the strategy live and re-tuned it yearly."
    },
    {
     "technical": "The engine already has the fold logic (`src/validation/walk_forward.py`: 36-month train, 6-month step, anchored). It is not yet how Strategy House judges a strategy. Proposal: adopt yearly walk-forward as the standard and re-run every runnable strategy, with this page's $10,000 view applied to the stitched out-of-sample record.",
     "plain": "We already have most of the code. The proposal is to make this the way every strategy is judged."
    }
   ]
